NAHPU can export the same project data in several ways, each for a different
job. All of them start from the project menu.

## Which export do I need?

| You want to… | Use | You get |
|---|---|---|
| Move a project to another device, or keep a full copy | `Export project` {% ref id="B" /%} | A project file that NAHPU can import or merge |
| Combine a teammate's project file with yours | `Merge project` {% ref id="A" /%} | Their records added to your project |
| Send records to a database, spreadsheet, or script | [Export records](/en/usages/export/export-records/) {% ref id="D" /%} | A CSV, TSV, Excel, or JSON table |
| Publish or archive data with its files | [Bundle records](/en/usages/export/export-bundles/) {% ref id="C" /%} | A Darwin Core or NAHPU Data Package |
| Print labels, tags, catalogs, or field books | [Export documents](/en/usages/export/export-documents/) {% ref id="E" /%} | A PDF |
| Report counts, such as specimens per species | [Export statistics](/en/usages/export/export-statistics/) | A summary table as CSV, TSV, Excel, or JSON |
| Pull out the project's photos | [Export images](/en/usages/export/export-images/) | One image, or a ZIP or TAR.GZ archive of many |
| Protect everything on this device | [`Backup database`](#backup-database) {% ref id="F" /%} | One archive of the whole installation |
| Share your settings and presets | [`Export user configs`](#export-user-configs) {% ref id="G" /%} | A settings file |

Only three of these can bring data back into NAHPU:

- a **project export**, through `Import project` on the home screen or
  `Merge project` in an existing project
  ([Project transfer](/en/usages/export/project-transfer/));
- a **NAHPU Data Package**, which carries the same project data;
- a **database backup**, which replaces the whole installation
  ([Restoring a database](#restoring-a-database)).

Record tables, Darwin Core packages, documents, and statistics are one-way:
NAHPU cannot rebuild a project from them. Importing a project-info JSON or a
project QR code creates only the project's name and details, without records or
media.

## Backup strategy {% #backup-strategy %}

Keep a checked copy of each field day's data somewhere other than the device
you work on. Two kinds of backup work together:

| Backup | How often | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| Project export | Every field day | One project, with its media in ZIP or TAR.GZ |
| Database backup | Weekly, and before importing, merging, or replacing data | Every project and setting on the device |

{% steps %}

1. Open the project menu and select `Export project`.
2. Check the record counts and any missing-file warnings. Choose ZIP or TAR.GZ
   to include media. `Light export` (JSON.GZ) holds records only, so it is not a
   full backup.
3. Save the file with the project name and date, and copy it to separate
   storage or another device.
4. Open the copy to make sure it is readable. Now and then, import it on another
   installation to test that recovery works.
5. At your regular checkpoint, also create a `Backup database`
   {% ref id="F" /%}. Keep the previous checked backup until the new one has
   been checked.

{% /steps %}

To recover a project, use `Import project` on the home screen, or
`Merge project` inside an existing project.

## Backup database {% #backup-database %}

`Backup database` saves the whole installation in one ZIP or TAR.GZ archive
{% ref id="H" /%}: the SQLite database plus the project, personnel, and
settings folders, including files no current record links to. A bare SQLite
file holds the records but not the media or other files.

{% steps %}

1. Open the project menu and select `Backup database`.
2. Choose the archive format, file name, and destination.
3. Select `Save backup` {% ref id="I" /%}.
4. Copy the archive off the device and check it before you delete an older
   backup.

{% /steps %}

## Export user configs {% #export-user-configs %}

`Export user configs` {% ref id="G" /%} saves your settings so another device,
or a future you, can work the same way. The file includes saved export presets,
document layouts, templates, and controlled lists.

{% steps %}

1. Open the `Dashboard` page.
2. Open the project menu and select `Export user configs`.
3. Choose a `Settings format`: `JSON (.json)` or the smaller
   `JSON.GZ (.json.gz)`.
4. Type a `File name` and choose the `Directory`.
5. Select `Export user configs`.

{% /steps %}

## Restoring a database {% #restoring-a-database %}

Restore a database to replace everything in NAHPU on this device with a backup.

{% steps %}

1. Open `Settings`.
2. Under `Database`, select `Replace database`.
3. Under `Replace with`, select `Browse` and choose a `.sqlite3` file, a ZIP
   archive, or a TAR.GZ archive.
4. Compare the two databases. NAHPU shows the backup's schema version, records,
   files, and size next to the current database. An older backup is upgraded
   after the restore. A file that is not a NAHPU database, or that comes from a
   newer NAHPU version, cannot be restored; update NAHPU first.
5. Keep `Back up current data first` on unless you are sure you no longer need
   the current data. NAHPU saves it as a TAR.GZ archive in its backup folder.
6. Select `Replace database` and confirm the warning.

{% /steps %}

What a restore does:

- It replaces the active database with the one in the backup.
- From an archive, it also copies the project files into NAHPU's documents
  folder and imports any settings in the archive. Personnel photos from older
  backups are moved to the personnel photo folder.
- A media or settings file with the same name as one in the archive is
  overwritten, without a second copy. Identical files are left alone.
- Files that exist only on the device are kept, and appear in later backups.

A restore cannot be undone, which is why `Back up current data first` should
stay on.

## Restoring user configs

Restore user configs to replace your settings with ones from another device or
backup.

{% steps %}

1. Open the project `Dashboard` menu.
2. Select `Import user configs`.
3. Choose a `.json` or `.json.gz` settings file.
4. Select `Replace` and confirm the warning.

{% /steps %}

This changes settings only, not project data. To restore both together, restore
a ZIP or TAR.GZ database archive. For how storage and compatibility work under
the hood, see [Data import and
export](../../contributing/code/data-import-export-overview).

## Archiving a project

An archive keeps a finished project usable years later, by people who may not
have worked on it. Store the data together with enough context to know what it
is, when it was made, with which software, and who is responsible for it.

Before archiving:

- Finish curating the data, and fix or write down known issues. See
  [Data Management](../../best-practices/data-management).
- Create a fresh database backup.
- Export user configs if the controlled lists, presets, templates, or layouts
  are needed to repeat the work.
- Create a full project export with `Light export` off, so media are included.
  Check the record counts and missing-media warnings before saving.
- Add record tables or a data package if the data will be analyzed, published,
  or reused outside NAHPU.

Name files so they explain themselves, with the project, export type, and date,
such as `BorneoMammals_project_20241015.tar.gz`. Keep the project export,
database backup, settings file, and a short readme together.

Store at least two copies in different places, and keep the original until the
archive opens correctly on another device. Record the archive date, contents,
storage locations, and any access restrictions. Open or test-restore the files
from time to time to confirm they are still readable. Keep digital files and
printed documents according to your institution's retention policies.

## Before sharing data

Before you send NAHPU data to a museum, a collaborator, or another device:

- Create a fresh database backup.
- Choose the right project file:
  - `Light export` (JSON.GZ) when the recipient needs records but not media,
    such as over a slow connection;
  - ZIP or TAR.GZ when media or a complete copy must travel with the project.
- Export user configs if the recipient needs your controlled lists, presets,
  templates, or layouts.
- Add the record files or documents the recipient asked for.
- Test that at least one way of restoring works before you delete anything
  from the original device.
